sections.body:
Real AI code is mostly data plumbing: fetch a request, reshape some JSON, call a model, return a result. The core toolkit:

```python
user = {"name": "Dana", "tier": "pro", "messages": 3}
tiers = ["free", "pro", "enterprise"]

if user["tier"] not in tiers:
    raise ValueError(f"unknown tier: {user['tier']}")

for i, t in enumerate(tiers):
    print(i, t)
```

Dictionaries and lists carry nearly all data. `if/else`, `for` loops, and `try/except` control the flow. F-strings build every message and log line. Functions package logic for reuse. Master these five things and you can read most production AI code today.

Everything you ship in this course lives inside functions, and every function that touches the outside world needs error handling:

```python
def extract_topic(text: str, max_words: int = 5) -> str:
    """Return a short topic label for a support message."""
    if not text.strip():
        raise ValueError("empty message")
    words = text.strip().split()
    return " ".join(words[:max_words]).lower()
```

Note the habits: type hints, a docstring, validation at the top, small pure logic. When an LLM writes code for you in later lessons, *these are the standards you review it against*. Defensive code isn't style — it's what survives contact with real users, and reviewers can smell the difference.

The real internet fails: 429 rate limits, 500s, 30-second timeouts. Production code retries:

```python
import time

def call_with_retry(fn, attempts=4):
    for i in range(attempts):
        try:
            return fn()
        except Exception as e:
            if i == attempts - 1:
                raise
            wait = 2 ** i  # 1s, 2s, 4s
            time.sleep(wait)
```

This is **exponential backoff**, and every serious AI codebase contains some version of it. The SDKs have built-in retry options too — know both. If your take-home assignment (Lesson 23) has no retry logic, reviewers notice. If it has it, they know you've been in production.

One call is a demo; systems are loops. Real pipelines call the model per item, track failures, log costs, and process in batches with a queue if volume grows. Sketch of the loop you'll build all course:

```python
results = []
for msg in messages:
    try:
        results.append(call_with_retry(lambda: classify(client, msg)))
    except Exception as e:
        results.append({"error": str(e), "input": msg})
```

Note the pattern: keep the input with the error so nothing is lost. Add `print(f"{i}/{len(messages)}")` progress logging and you have the skeleton of every batch job, evaluator, and agent step you'll write in this course.

Give the model a menu of tools and it chooses: that's **function calling**. You describe each function — name, purpose, parameters as JSON schema — and the model replies not with text but with a tool request. Your code executes it and feeds the result back:

```python
tools = [{
    "type": "function",
    "function": {
        "name": "get_order",
        "description": "Fetch order status by ID",
        "parameters": {"properties": {
            "order_id": {"type": "string"}}, "required": ["order_id"]},
    },
}]
```

The loop — model picks a tool → you run it → result goes back → model continues — is the whole mechanism of ChatGPT's own features and every agent you'll ever build. Everything else is orchestration around this loop. Tool use is 11% of agentic postings; this is that skill, at the metal.

**Model Context Protocol** is 2026's answer to tool chaos: a standard way to expose tools, resources, and prompts to any MCP-capable client — one server, every model. At 27% of agentic postings, it's the fastest-rising skill in the index. You can write an MCP server in a few lines and your tools instantly plug into Claude, Cursor, and countless agents:

```python
from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

mcp = FastMCP("support")

@mcp.tool()
def get_order(order_id: str) -> dict:
    """Fetch order status by ID."""
    return orders.get(order_id, {"status": "unknown"})
```

Mental model: function calling is the *mechanism*; MCP is the *port standard* — USB for AI tools. Interviewers increasingly ask 'what is MCP and why does it matter?' — and now you can whiteboard it.
